# .NET/C# Standards

## Pre-Check: C# Version Compatibility Gate

> **MANDATORY before applying any code pattern from this rule.**
>
> 1. Find the nearest `*.csproj` file for the file being edited.
> 2. Read `<TargetFramework>` and (if present) `<LangVersion>`.
> 3. Use the table below to determine the applicable C# version.
> 4. **Skip any section whose version tag exceeds the project's C# version.**

| TargetFramework | Default C# | Available features |
|-----------------|-----------|-------------------|
| `net48` / `net472` / `net461` | 7.3 | No records, no primary constructors, no nullable annotations (unless `<LangVersion>` overridden), no collection expressions, no pattern matching `is not`, no `switch` expressions |
| `net48` + `<LangVersion>8.0</LangVersion>` | 8.0 | + Nullable annotations, `switch` expressions, `using` declarations, default interface methods |
| `net48` + `<LangVersion>9.0</LangVersion>` | 9.0 | + Records, pattern matching (`is not`, `and`, `or`), target-typed `new()` |
| `net6.0` / `net7.0` | 10 / 11 | + Global usings, file-scoped namespaces, raw string literals |
| `net8.0` | 12 | + Primary constructors, collection expressions (`[]`), `ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace` |
| `net9.0` | 13 | + All current features |

**If `<LangVersion>` is not set**, use the default for the target framework.
**If in doubt**, prefer the traditional (lower-version) pattern — it always works.

---

## Compatibility Note

> These standards target **modern C#** (C# 10+, .NET 6+). For **.NET Framework 4.x**
> projects (C# 7.3), skip sections marked with **(C# 10+)** or **(C# 12+)**.
> Check the project's `*.csproj` `<TargetFramework>` and `<LangVersion>` to
> determine which features are available.

## Core Principles
- **SOLID Principles**: Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, Dependency Inversion
- **Modern C#**: Use modern C# features when the target framework supports them (see Compatibility Note)
- **Performance**: Optimize for memory efficiency and execution speed
- **Maintainability**: Write self-documenting, testable code

## Naming Conventions
```csharp
// PascalCase for public members
public class ElementProcessor
{
    public void ProcessElements() { }
    public string ElementName { get; set; }
}

// camelCase for private fields and local variables
private readonly IElementService _elementService;
private bool _isProcessing;

// Interfaces prefixed with 'I'
public interface IElementProcessor
{
    Task<ProcessResult> ProcessAsync(IEnumerable<Element> elements);
}

// Constants and enums
public const string DefaultElementName = "Unnamed Element";
public enum ProcessingStatus { NotStarted, InProgress, Completed, Failed }
```

## Modern C# Features

> **(C# 12+)** The features below require .NET 8+ / C# 12+.
> For .NET Framework 4.x projects, use traditional constructors and classes instead.
```csharp
// Primary constructors (C# 12+) - Optional, not required
// Use when it simplifies code, traditional constructors are also fine
public class ElementService(ILogger<ElementService> logger, IConfiguration configuration) : IElementService
{
    public async Task<ProcessResult> ProcessAsync(Element element)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Processing element {ElementId}", element.Id);
        // Implementation
    }
}

// Traditional constructor (still widely used in codebase)
public class ElementService : IElementService
{
    private readonly ILogger<ElementService> _logger;
    private readonly IConfiguration _configuration;
    
    public ElementService(ILogger<ElementService> logger, IConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _logger = logger;
        _configuration = configuration;
    }
}

// Record types for immutable data (C# 9+ / .NET 5+)
public record ElementInfo(
    ElementId Id,
    string Name,
    string Category,
    BoundingBoxXYZ BoundingBox
);

// .NET Framework 4.x alternative: use a sealed class with readonly properties
// public sealed class ElementInfo
// {
//     public ElementId Id { get; }
//     public string Name { get; }
//     public ElementInfo(ElementId id, string name) { Id = id; Name = name; }
// }

// Record with validation
public record CreateElementRequest(string Name, string Category)
{
    public CreateElementRequest(string Name, string Category) : this(Name, Category)
    {
        ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace(Name);
        ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace(Category);
    }
}
```

## Nullable Reference Types

> **(C# 8+)** Nullable annotations require C# 8+ (.NET Core 3.1+ or
> .NET Framework 4.8 with `<LangVersion>8.0</LangVersion>` and `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>`).
```csharp
// Enable in project file
<PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
</PropertyGroup>

// Use nullable annotations
public class ElementValidator
{
    public ValidationResult? ValidateElement(Element? element)
    {
        if (element is null)
            return null;
            
        return new ValidationResult(element.IsValidObject, GetValidationMessage(element));
    }
    
    private string GetValidationMessage(Element element) => 
        element.IsValidObject ? "Valid" : "Invalid element";
}

// Note: Not all projects have nullable enabled yet
// Apply nullable annotations when working on new code or refactoring
```

## Async/Await Patterns
```csharp
// Always use ConfigureAwait(false) in library code
public async Task<ProcessResult> ProcessElementsAsync(IEnumerable<Element> elements)
{
    var results = new List<ElementResult>();
    
    foreach (var element in elements)
    {
        var result = await ProcessSingleElementAsync(element).ConfigureAwait(false);
        results.Add(result);
    }
    
    return new ProcessResult(results);
}

// Use ValueTask for frequently called methods
public ValueTask<bool> IsElementValidAsync(Element element)
{
    if (element.IsValidObject)
        return ValueTask.FromResult(true);
        
    return ValidateElementSlowAsync(element);
}

// Cancellation support
public async Task<ProcessResult> ProcessElementsAsync(
    IEnumerable<Element> elements, 
    CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
{
    foreach (var element in elements)
    {
        cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        // Process element
    }
}
```

## Error Handling
```csharp
// Use specific exception types
public class ElementProcessingException : Exception
{
    public ElementId ElementId { get; }
    
    public ElementProcessingException(ElementId elementId, string message) 
        : base(message)
    {
        ElementId = elementId;
    }
}

// Guard clauses for validation (C# 10+ for ThrowIfNull; .NET Framework: use manual null check)
public void ProcessElement(Element element)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(element);
    
    if (!element.IsValidObject)
        throw new ArgumentException("Element is not valid", nameof(element));
        
    // Processing logic
}

// Result pattern for operations that can fail (C# 9+ for records; .NET Framework: use class)
public record Result<T>
{
    public bool IsSuccess { get; init; }
    public T? Value { get; init; }
    public string? Error { get; init; }
    
    public static Result<T> Success(T value) => new() { IsSuccess = true, Value = value };
    public static Result<T> Failure(string error) => new() { IsSuccess = false, Error = error };
}
```

## Dependency Injection
```csharp
// Extension method for service registration
public static class ServiceCollectionExtensions
{
    public static IServiceCollection AddElementServices(this IServiceCollection services)
    {
        services.AddScoped<IElementProcessor, ElementProcessor>();
        services.AddScoped<IElementValidator, ElementValidator>();
        services.AddSingleton<IElementCache, ElementCache>();
        
        return services;
    }
}

// Service interfaces for testability
public interface IElementProcessor
{
    Task<ProcessResult> ProcessAsync(IEnumerable<Element> elements, CancellationToken cancellationToken = default);
    Task<ValidationResult> ValidateAsync(Element element);
